std.crypto.hmac — HMAC-SHA-256
==============================

HMAC as specified in [RFC 2104](https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc2104),
instantiated with SHA-256. Produces a 256-bit (32-byte) message
authentication code.

HMAC is a *message authentication code* — given a shared secret key, it
proves a message was generated by someone who knows the key. It is not
a hash function (there is no public verifiability) and not encryption
(the message is not hidden). HMAC's purpose is integrity:
the receiver, also knowing the key, recomputes the MAC and rejects
the message if it doesn't match.

The construction wraps the underlying hash $H$ (SHA-256 here) with
two passes that each prepend a key-derived 64-byte block:

$$
\mathrm{HMAC}_K(m) = H\big( (K' \oplus \text{opad}) \;\Vert\; H( (K' \oplus \text{ipad}) \;\Vert\; m ) \big)
$$

where:

- $K'$ is the key normalized to one block (64 bytes for SHA-256):
  hashed down if it was too long, zero-padded if too short.
- `ipad` = `0x36` repeated 64 times.
- `opad` = `0x5c` repeated 64 times.
- `‖` is concatenation.

The two distinct pad constants ensure the inner and outer hash inputs
differ; the wrapping defeats length-extension attacks against
Merkle–Damgård hashes (the very class to which SHA-256 belongs).

Dependencies: `std.crypto.sha256`.

    module std.crypto.hmac

    import std.crypto.sha256.{sha256, sha256_hw}


Constants
---------

The block size is the underlying hash's input block size (64 bytes for
SHA-256). The hash size is the digest length (32 bytes).

    val BLOCK_SIZE = 64
    val HASH_SIZE = 32


`hmac_sha256` — software MAC
----------------------------

`hmac_sha256(key, msg, out)` computes the HMAC-SHA-256 of `msg` under
`key` and writes the 32-byte tag to `out`. The tag is deterministic in
both inputs.

    hmac_sha256(key: []byte, msg: []byte, out: []byte)

**Step 1 — normalize the key to one block.** Keys longer than the
block are hashed down (per RFC 2104); keys shorter than the block are
zero-padded by the `var k0: [64]byte = ""` initializer.

        var k0: [64]byte = ""

        if len(key) > BLOCK_SIZE
            sha256(key, k0[:])
        else
            for i in 0..<len(key)
                k0[i] = key[i]

**Step 2 — XOR the normalized key with each pad.** `ipad` and `opad`
are the inner and outer keying blocks respectively; they will be
prepended to the inner and outer hash inputs.

        var ipad: [64]byte
        var opad: [64]byte

        for i in 0..<BLOCK_SIZE
            ipad[i] = k0[i] ^ 0x36u8
            opad[i] = k0[i] ^ 0x5cu8

**Step 3 — inner hash:** $\mathrm{inner} = H(\mathrm{ipad} \;\Vert\; m)$.
The inner buffer is sized exactly to the message length so there is no
fixed cap on `len(msg)`.

        val inner_len = BLOCK_SIZE + len(msg)
        val inner_buf = (new [inner_len]byte)[:]
        for i in 0..<BLOCK_SIZE
            inner_buf[i] = ipad[i]
        for i in 0..<len(msg)
            inner_buf[BLOCK_SIZE + i] = msg[i]

        var inner_hash: [32]byte
        sha256(inner_buf, inner_hash[:])

**Step 4 — outer hash:** $\mathrm{out} = H(\mathrm{opad} \;\Vert\; \mathrm{inner})$.
The outer buffer is exactly 96 bytes — `BLOCK_SIZE + HASH_SIZE` — and
needs no allocation.

        var outer_buf: [96]byte
        for i in 0..<BLOCK_SIZE
            outer_buf[i] = opad[i]
        for i in 0..<HASH_SIZE
            outer_buf[BLOCK_SIZE + i] = inner_hash[i]

        sha256(outer_buf[:], out)


`hmac_sha256_hw` — hardware-accelerated MAC
-------------------------------------------

Identical structure to `hmac_sha256`, but routes both compression
calls through `sha256_hw` (the MMIO accelerator). The two functions
produce bit-identical output; the hardware path only changes which
silicon does the SHA-256 work.

    hmac_sha256_hw(key: []byte, msg: []byte, out: []byte)
        var k0: [64]byte = ""

        if len(key) > BLOCK_SIZE
            sha256_hw(key, k0[:])
        else
            for i in 0..<len(key)
                k0[i] = key[i]

        var ipad: [64]byte
        var opad: [64]byte

        for i in 0..<BLOCK_SIZE
            ipad[i] = k0[i] ^ 0x36u8
            opad[i] = k0[i] ^ 0x5cu8

        val inner_len = BLOCK_SIZE + len(msg)
        val inner_buf = (new [inner_len]byte)[:]
        for i in 0..<BLOCK_SIZE
            inner_buf[i] = ipad[i]
        for i in 0..<len(msg)
            inner_buf[BLOCK_SIZE + i] = msg[i]

        var inner_hash: [32]byte
        sha256_hw(inner_buf, inner_hash[:])

        var outer_buf: [96]byte
        for i in 0..<BLOCK_SIZE
            outer_buf[i] = opad[i]
        for i in 0..<HASH_SIZE
            outer_buf[BLOCK_SIZE + i] = inner_hash[i]

        sha256_hw(outer_buf[:], out)


Tests
=====

    import std.debug.{assert}


### RFC 4231 Test Case 1 — short key, "Hi There"

Key = 20 bytes of `0x0b`, message = `"Hi There"`. From
[RFC 4231 §4.2](https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc4231#section-4.2).

Expected: `b0344c61 d8db3853 5ca8afce af0bf12b 881dc200 c9833da7 26e9376c 2e32cff7`.

    #test
    test_hmac_rfc4231_tc1() -> unit
        var key: [20]byte
        for i in 0..<20
            key[i] = 0x0bu8

        val msg: [8]byte = "Hi There"
        var out: [32]byte
        hmac_sha256(key[:], msg[:], out[:])

        val expected: [32]byte = [0xb0, 0x34, 0x4c, 0x61, 0xd8, 0xdb, 0x38, 0x53, 0x5c, 0xa8, 0xaf, 0xce, 0xaf, 0x0b, 0xf1, 0x2b, 0x88, 0x1d, 0xc2, 0x00, 0xc9, 0x83, 0x3d, 0xa7, 0x26, 0xe9, 0x37, 0x6c, 0x2e, 0x32, 0xcf, 0xf7]

        for i in 0..<32
            assert(out[i] == expected[i], s"TC1 byte $i: got ${int(out[i])} want ${int(expected[i])}")


### RFC 4231 Test Case 2 — text key, text message

Key = `"Jefe"`, message = `"what do ya want for nothing?"`.

Expected: `5bdcc146 bf60754e 6a042426 089575c7 5a003f08 9d273983 9dec58b9 64ec3843`.

    #test
    test_hmac_rfc4231_tc2() -> unit
        val key: [4]byte = "Jefe"
        val msg: [28]byte = "what do ya want for nothing?"
        var out: [32]byte
        hmac_sha256(key[:], msg[:], out[:])

        val expected: [32]byte = [0x5b, 0xdc, 0xc1, 0x46, 0xbf, 0x60, 0x75, 0x4e, 0x6a, 0x04, 0x24, 0x26, 0x08, 0x95, 0x75, 0xc7, 0x5a, 0x00, 0x3f, 0x08, 0x9d, 0x27, 0x39, 0x83, 0x9d, 0xec, 0x58, 0xb9, 0x64, 0xec, 0x38, 0x43]

        for i in 0..<32
            assert(out[i] == expected[i], s"TC2 byte $i: got ${int(out[i])} want ${int(expected[i])}")


### RFC 4231 Test Case 3 — short key, long message

Key = 20 bytes of `0xaa`, message = 50 bytes of `0xdd`. Exercises the
multi-block message path inside SHA-256.

Expected: `773ea91e 36800e46 854db8eb d09181a7 2959098b 3ef8c122 d9635514 ced565fe`.

    #test
    test_hmac_rfc4231_tc3() -> unit
        var key: [20]byte
        for i in 0..<20
            key[i] = 0xaau8

        var msg: [50]byte
        for i in 0..<50
            msg[i] = 0xddu8

        var out: [32]byte
        hmac_sha256(key[:], msg[:], out[:])

        val expected: [32]byte = [0x77, 0x3e, 0xa9, 0x1e, 0x36, 0x80, 0x0e, 0x46, 0x85, 0x4d, 0xb8, 0xeb, 0xd0, 0x91, 0x81, 0xa7, 0x29, 0x59, 0x09, 0x8b, 0x3e, 0xf8, 0xc1, 0x22, 0xd9, 0x63, 0x55, 0x14, 0xce, 0xd5, 0x65, 0xfe]

        for i in 0..<32
            assert(out[i] == expected[i], s"TC3 byte $i: got ${int(out[i])} want ${int(expected[i])}")


### Short key and message — `key="key"`, `msg="msg"`

Cross-reference vector against `openssl dgst -sha256 -mac HMAC -macopt key:key`.

Expected: `2d93cbc1 be167bcb 1637a4a2 3cbff01a 7878f0c5 0ee83395 4ea5221b b1b8c628`.

    #test
    test_hmac_short() -> unit
        val key: [3]byte = "key"
        val msg: [3]byte = "msg"
        var out: [32]byte
        hmac_sha256(key[:], msg[:], out[:])

        val expected: [32]byte = [0x2d, 0x93, 0xcb, 0xc1, 0xbe, 0x16, 0x7b, 0xcb, 0x16, 0x37, 0xa4, 0xa2, 0x3c, 0xbf, 0xf0, 0x1a, 0x78, 0x78, 0xf0, 0xc5, 0x0e, 0xe8, 0x33, 0x95, 0x4e, 0xa5, 0x22, 0x1b, 0xb1, 0xb8, 0xc6, 0x28]

        for i in 0..<32
            assert(out[i] == expected[i], s"short byte $i: got ${int(out[i])} want ${int(expected[i])}")


### Empty message — `key="secret"`, `msg=""`

The HMAC of an empty message is still a meaningful, key-dependent tag.

Expected: `f9e66e17 9b6747ae 54108f82 f8ade8b3 c25d76fd 30afde6c 395822c5 30196169`.

    #test
    test_hmac_empty_message() -> unit
        val key: [6]byte = "secret"
        val empty: [0]byte = ""
        var out: [32]byte
        hmac_sha256(key[:], empty[:], out[:])

        val expected: [32]byte = [0xf9, 0xe6, 0x6e, 0x17, 0x9b, 0x67, 0x47, 0xae, 0x54, 0x10, 0x8f, 0x82, 0xf8, 0xad, 0xe8, 0xb3, 0xc2, 0x5d, 0x76, 0xfd, 0x30, 0xaf, 0xde, 0x6c, 0x39, 0x58, 0x22, 0xc5, 0x30, 0x19, 0x61, 0x69]

        for i in 0..<32
            assert(out[i] == expected[i], s"empty byte $i: got ${int(out[i])} want ${int(expected[i])}")


### Determinism — same inputs produce identical output

    #test
    test_hmac_deterministic() -> unit
        val key: [4]byte = "test"
        val msg: [5]byte = "hello"
        var out1: [32]byte
        var out2: [32]byte
        hmac_sha256(key[:], msg[:], out1[:])
        hmac_sha256(key[:], msg[:], out2[:])

        for i in 0..<32
            assert(out1[i] == out2[i], "deterministic")


Design notes
------------

**Dynamic inner buffer.** The inner buffer is `(new [inner_len]byte)`,
sized exactly to `BLOCK_SIZE + len(msg)`, so there is no fixed message
size limit. The outer buffer is fixed at 96 bytes because its inputs
are always one block plus one digest.

**Key normalization.** RFC 2104 specifies that keys longer than the
hash's block size are first hashed down; keys shorter are zero-padded.
The `var k0: [64]byte = ""` declaration establishes the all-zero
baseline so the short-key branch only needs to copy the actual key
bytes — the rest is already zero.

**Constant-time tag comparison is the caller's responsibility.** This
module produces a tag; comparing two tags without leaking timing
information through an early-out is the caller's job. See the AEAD
module for an example.
